{"name":"VIC 20 Blue","hex":"#c7ffff","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Virgin Islands Creole."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A victim of a homicide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vic"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"VIC-20","blocks":[{"text":"The VIC-20 is an 8-bit entry level home computer that was sold by Commodore Business Machines. The VIC-20 was announced in September 1980, roughly three years after Commodore's first personal computer, the PET. The VIC-20 was the first computer of any description to sell one million units, and sales of the VIC-20 eventually reached 2.5 million units."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Commodore-VIC-20-FL.jpg/330px-Commodore-VIC-20-FL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VIC-20"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/VIC-20_Display.jpg/500px-VIC-20_Display.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/VIC-20_Display.jpg/1280px-VIC-20_Display.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/VIC-20_Display.jpg/500px-VIC-20_Display.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An early revision of the VIC-20 with several accessories. The  composite output allowed it to be connected to standard television sets of its era.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVIC-20_Display.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/ViC20_Cartridge.jpg/330px-ViC20_Cartridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/ViC20_Cartridge.jpg/330px-ViC20_Cartridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Clowns game on a ROM cartridge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViC20_Cartridge.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/VIC_20_Splash_Screen_Screenshot.gif/500px-VIC_20_Splash_Screen_Screenshot.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/VIC_20_Splash_Screen_Screenshot.gif/500px-VIC_20_Splash_Screen_Screenshot.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Th